# Your own private identifiers — copy this file to `private-identifiers.txt` and edit.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
# The validator refuses to let private names leak into a skill file. Some of those names are
# private for everybody — a home directory, a personal path — and those are built in. The rest
# are private to YOU: your company's repo names, an internal package scope, the name of a
# scaffolding script nobody outside your team has heard of. Those cannot be built in, because
# this validator ships. Put them here instead.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE
# A guard against publishing private names must not publish them itself. Anything written
# directly into the validator would travel with it to every clone — which is the disclosure the
# check exists to prevent. This file is gitignored, so your copy stays on your machine.
#
# It is optional. No file is the normal case and the validator simply carries on with its
# built-in patterns.
#
# FORMAT
#   - One Python regular expression per line.
#   - Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored.
#   - A line that will not compile is a hard error, never a silent skip: a guard that quietly
#     drops a pattern is worse than no guard, because you believe you are covered.
#
# \b means "word boundary" — it stops `acme` from also matching `acmesoft`. Escape dots
# (`\.`), since an unescaped `.` matches any character.
#
# EXAMPLES — delete these and add your own.
#
# \bacme_internal\b        a private repository name
# @acme                    an internal npm scope
# \bdeploy-prod\.sh\b      an internal script name
# \bacme\.corp\b           an internal hostname
#
# CHECK IT WORKS
#   python3 scripts/validate_cross_runtime.py skills/
# Add one of your names to a skill file, run that, and confirm it fails. A check you have
# never seen fail is a check you have not tested.
